The images beside this file - floor.png, wall.png, metal.png, trim.png,
wood.png and the *_normal.png maps generated from them - come from the FoxTex
texture pack and are used under CC0 1.0 Universal (Public Domain Dedication).

    Pack:   FoxTex
    Author: Foxhead
    Source: https://foxh3ad.itch.io/foxtexcom

Where the CC0 claim comes from, honestly: the pack itself carries no license
text anywhere in it. The author confirmed CC0 in a reply to a question on the
download page around the middle of 2026 and said the page information would be
updated. If the page now states it, cite the page here and delete this
paragraph; if it does not, this paragraph is the evidence and should stay.

The full CC0 1.0 legal code is at:

    https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode

CC0 1.0 in short: the person who associated a work with this deed has dedicated
it to the public domain by waiving all of their rights to the work worldwide
under copyright law, including all related and neighboring rights, to the
extent allowed by law. The work can be copied, modified, distributed and
performed, even for commercial purposes, all without asking permission.

The *_normal.png files are not the author's work. They were generated from the
albedo images in this directory, in three steps, and the recipe is written down
because nothing downstream can tell a measured normal from a guessed one:

  1. the luminance is thresholded into a height field, with everything more
     than 2.2 standard deviations below the image's own mean counting as fully
     deep and everything less than 0.8 below it counting as flat. That is the
     step that matters: using luminance *directly* as a height turns every
     speck of the albedo's mottling into a facet, and the first attempt at
     these did exactly that - fourteen times the per-pixel variation of a map
     that worked;
  2. the height is blurred by two to three pixels, which is what turns a step
     into a bevel;
  3. the gradient is taken, wrapping at the edges because these textures tile.

It is a guess about shape made from a photograph of color, and it is right only
in so far as darker means deeper on these particular surfaces.

grate.png is not the author's work either, and is not a photograph of anything.
It is arithmetic, written down here for the reason above - nothing downstream
can tell a generated picture from a scanned one, and this is the only place the
recipe can live:

    128 by 128, RGBA. Bars 12 pixels wide on a 64-pixel pitch, offset by half a
    bar, so a texel is inside a bar when either (x + 6) % 64 < 12 or
    (y + 6) % 64 < 12. Alpha is 255 there and 0 everywhere else, which is a
    third of the image solid. The offset is what makes one tile of this a
    closed grid: without it the bars start at a tile's edge, so a face wearing
    one tile shows two sides of a frame and not four.

    The color is a painted steel, (150, 155, 164) in sRGB, multiplied by a
    shade that runs from 0.72 at a bar's edge to 1.28 down its middle - a
    triangle across the bar's width rather than a flat slab, which is what
    makes a bar read as round rather than as a stripe. Where two bars cross,
    the larger of the two shades wins.

Two things about it are worth knowing before it is changed. The alpha is
binary rather than feathered, because what reads it is a cutoff at a half and a
feathered edge would only move where that cutoff lands. And a third of the
image being solid means the *mip chain* averages towards an alpha of about 87,
which is under the cutoff - so far enough away this grate thins out and
disappears rather than filling in. That is the opposite of the usual complaint
about cutouts and it has the same one cause.
